WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.740
- Okay, I have a question for you.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.080
Listen to these clips
from three different songs

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.271
and try and guess what
they all have in common.

00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:09.688
(jazz music)

00:00:11.161 --> 00:00:13.828
(country music)

00:00:15.074 --> 00:00:17.491
(rock music)

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:19.783
Could you figure it out?

00:00:19.783 --> 00:00:21.356
Whether directly or indirectly,

00:00:21.356 --> 00:00:23.160
all of these songs were inspired

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.190
in some way by blues music.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.700
But how are all these songs
that sound so different

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.480
influenced by the same music,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.612
and what makes blues music the blues?

00:00:31.612 --> 00:00:34.279
(soulful music)

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.280
- Because if you don't like the blues,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.610
you don't like your mama.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.630
'Cause it's the ruler of music, man.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.910
It's like cooking potatoes.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.510
You may fry 'em, you may bake 'em,

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.533
you may cut 'em up, you may smash 'em,

00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.620
but they're still potatoes.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.700
- Gritty guitar solos, wailing harmonicas,

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.683
and soulful lyrics about life's hardships

00:00:54.683 --> 00:00:57.960
are all part of what makes
people love the blues.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.340
But how did this music start,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.730
and why is it so important
to American music?

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.570
The answer to this question can be found

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.400
deep in the Mississippi
Delta of the American south,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.660
where the blues was born
some time in the late 1800s.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.790
- Even after slavery officially ended,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.620
poverty and unjust laws kept
people from full freedom.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.410
Many former slaves stayed in the south

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.630
and continued to work
in difficult conditions.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.480
People sang spirituals at church

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.840
and work songs in the field

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.550
to help deal with the daily struggles.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.560
Over time, this music developed

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.200
into what people now call the blues.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.510
- Most blues musicians
were farm workers by day

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:34.230
and would play at blues bars
called juke joints by night.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.380
The earliest form of the blues

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.062
was often referred to as
country blues or Delta blues,

00:01:38.062 --> 00:01:41.470
and this style usually had a solo singer

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:43.930
who would also either play the harmonica

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.870
or bottleneck slide guitar.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.810
Bottleneck slide guitar is when a musician

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.640
presses a hard object
across multiple strings

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.100
on a guitar and slides
it down the fingerboard.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.680
Sometimes they would use a steel bar,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.810
a knife, or a bottleneck,

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.959
and this would create a
smooth, wailing type of sound.

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:05.310
(guitar music)

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.860
The Delta blues also emphasized
rhythm, finger-style guitar,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.490
and reflective lyrics about the hard lives

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.760
of African American farmers in the South.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.380
As blues music grew in popularity,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.409
some musicians like Charley Patton,

00:02:17.409 --> 00:02:19.360
Eddie James "Son" House, Jr.,

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.720
and Robert Johnson became famous

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.710
and toured around the delta region.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.640
Later, blues musicians started performing

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.112
in Memphis, Tennessee to
establish their careers.

00:02:28.112 --> 00:02:30.461
This included famous
blues men like B.B. King

00:02:30.461 --> 00:02:32.540
and John Lee Hooker.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.670
- During what's known
as the Great Migration,

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.040
from 1910 to 1970, millions
of African Americans

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.130
tried to escape the racism
and Jim Crow laws of the south

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.700
by moving to northern cities.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.330
Many moved to the city of
Chicago, in particular.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.300
(blues music)

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.036
Blues musicians like
Howlin' Wolf, Willie Dixon,

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.130
and Muddy Waters made this journey

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.800
and brought the blues with them.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.670
They also developed a
new Chicago blues sound.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.100
Chicago blues was basically
the same style of blues

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.955
played in the delta, except
played more energetically,

00:03:00.955 --> 00:03:03.049
with an electric guitar,

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.720
sometimes a bass guitar, piano, and drums.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.260
Listen to this clip from Robert
Johnson's delta blues style

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.650
song, "I Believe I'll Dust
My Broom," recorded in 1936.

00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:17.689
("I Believe I'll Dust My
Broom" by Robert Johnson)

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.650
Now, listen to a 1966
version of the same song

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.350
in a more Chicago style by Howlin' Wolf.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:30.350
("I Believe I'll Dust My
Broom" by Howlin' Wolf)

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.140
Now the Chicago style would later

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.240
have an influence on rock and roll,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.760
but we'll talk more about that later.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.320
What gives you the blues, Nahre?

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.208
When they run out of peanut butter jelly?

00:03:39.208 --> 00:03:41.650
- (laughs) Oh, wow, you
know me really well now.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.480
How much experience do you
have playing the blues?

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.310
- That is a very interesting question

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.290
because it's also asking me

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.980
how much experience do
I have playing gospel,

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.138
rock and roll, R&amp;B, soul-

00:03:55.138 --> 00:03:56.028
- Because it's all intertwined.

00:03:56.028 --> 00:03:56.890
- Yeah.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:58.530
- I actually did an episode on my channel

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.493
on the blues a year ago.

00:04:00.493 --> 00:04:03.180
(electronic keyboard music)

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.583
And since then, I've
learned a lot about blues,

00:04:05.583 --> 00:04:08.900
about the blues as a
genre and also the form.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.517
- How would you say blues music

00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:15.970
and your realm of classical
music are related?

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:19.860
- The way that melody
is formed over chords,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.060
how it's elaborating
upon different chords,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.790
different chord progression,

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.230
I think that's where the similarity is.

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.609
- Wow, right.

00:04:27.609 --> 00:04:30.350
- So what is it that makes
blues music sound bluesy?

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:31.460
Well, let's take a look at the scale

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.600
that's most commonly found in blues songs,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.937
which is a six-note scale
based off of a five-note scale.

00:04:36.937 --> 00:04:38.620
That five-note scale either being

00:04:39.562 --> 00:04:41.890
the major pentatonic scale
(plays scale on piano)

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.437
or the minor one (plays minor scale),

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.140
and as you can hear,
these five-note scales

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.130
are quite different

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.780
than the seven-note scales
(plays seven-note scale)

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:54.410
that you most commonly
hear in western music.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:55.480
Now going back to

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.420
the major pentatonic scale (plays scale),

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.680
to that we're going to add

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.930
a flat three from the
root note (plays flat).

00:05:01.958 --> 00:05:02.947
That will sound like this.

00:05:02.947 --> 00:05:05.947
(plays piano scale)

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:08.470
To the minor one (plays scale),

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:10.783
we're going to add

00:05:10.783 --> 00:05:13.810
the flat five from the
root note (plays flat),

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:15.459
and that'll sound like this.

00:05:15.459 --> 00:05:18.459
(plays piano scale)

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.690
Another important part
of the blues is the form,

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:25.690
and most blues will follow a
12-bar blues chord progression,

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:28.070
which is based around
three different chords.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.150
Those chords in C Major
will be C (plays chord),

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.940
the one chord, F (plays
chord) the four chord,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.660
and G (plays chord) the five chord.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.293
And these chords will be
repeated in a specific order,

00:05:40.293 --> 00:05:43.610
most commonly with a flat seven as well.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.555
So a C7 (plays chord), F7 (plays chord),

00:05:45.555 --> 00:05:47.990
and G7 (plays chord).

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.818
And that'll sound like this.

00:05:49.818 --> 00:05:52.060
(plays chords in succession)

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.743
C7 for four measures.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:55.813
Three.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.193
Four.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:00.627
F7 for two.

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:05.773
C7 for two.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:10.503
G7 for one.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:15.083
F7 for one, and back to C7 for two.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.160
Of course, improvisation is
a huge part of the blues,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.920
and no tune is complete without it,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.732
so I'm going to give it a shot.

00:06:23.732 --> 00:06:26.732
(plays blues music)

00:06:49.637 --> 00:06:50.550
(chuckles)

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.050
Is there something that is similar to

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.150
what a blue note is in terms of pitches

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:57.718
in the context of just drums?

00:06:57.718 --> 00:06:59.190
- Absolutely.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.700
With pitch, it's like if you're playing

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.810
in between a C and a C sharp.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.090
There's a note in between
the C and C sharp.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:07.850
A blue note in rhythm would be like

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.840
if it's directly on the beat

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.840
or there's notes in
between the quantized beats

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.860
that you can still ride with.
- Ah!

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.570
- Another important element of blues

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:22.240
is its A-A-B lyrical structure.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.767
Let's go back to the Robert Johnson track,

00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:25.379
"I Believe I'll Dust My Broom."

00:07:25.379 --> 00:07:29.132
♪ I'm gonna get up in the morning' ♪

00:07:29.132 --> 00:07:31.610
♪ I believe I'll dust my broom ♪

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.177
Now, do you know what lyrics come next?

00:07:34.177 --> 00:07:37.537
♪ I'm gonna get up in the morning' ♪

00:07:37.537 --> 00:07:39.616
♪ I believe I'll dust my broom ♪

00:07:39.616 --> 00:07:41.460
It's the first line repeated.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.140
This A-A-B lyrical structure
is very common in the blues.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.670
It's inspired by the call
and response style of singing

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.370
in African American
spirituals and work songs.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:53.120
In the blues, the first
line is often repeated,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.185
and the third line answers.

00:07:55.185 --> 00:07:59.312
♪ Girlfriend the black
man you've been lovin' ♪

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:02.011
♪ Girlfriend can get my room ♪

00:08:02.011 --> 00:08:04.027
Of course, what the lyrics
are about is also important.

00:08:04.027 --> 00:08:09.027
- Now true blues, the
lyrics are absolutely true.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.470
Saying about life experience.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:12.600
(plays chord on guitar)

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.805
Sometimes good, sometimes bad,

00:08:15.805 --> 00:08:17.360
but they didn't lie.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.110
- As blues musicians moved from the south

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.800
and took their music to
different part of the U.S.,

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:23.870
the blues mixed with other musical styles

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:27.270
from those regions and helped
inspire many new genres

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:29.010
- The 12-bar blues chord progression

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.960
became one of the most popular forms

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.270
for early jazz improvisation.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.110
Jazz performers like Louis Armstrong,

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:36.920
Duke Ellington, and Miles Davis

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:38.810
were all influenced by the blues,

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.691
often using a 12-bar
structure for their songs.

00:08:41.691 --> 00:08:44.108
(jazz music)

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.740
The 12-bar structure was also

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.890
a major influence on rock and roll.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:52.760
Some even call rock and
roll blues with a backbeat,

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.860
and Elvis Presley
probably had this in mind

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.810
when he recorded his
version of a blues song

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.334
by Big Mama Thornton called, "Hound Dog."

00:09:00.334 --> 00:09:04.584
("Hound Dog" by Big Mama Thornton)

00:09:06.034 --> 00:09:10.530
("Hound Dog by Elvis Presley)

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.230
- Blues also inspired
other rock and pop artists,

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:17.544
such as ("Johnny B.Goode" by Chuck Berry)

00:09:17.544 --> 00:09:20.891
("Can't Buy Me Love" by the Beatles)

00:09:20.891 --> 00:09:24.779
("Sunshine of Your Love" by Cream)

00:09:24.779 --> 00:09:27.970
("Ball and Biscuit" by The White Stripes)

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.310
All of these songs use some form

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.780
of the 12-bar structure
or the blues scale.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.800
The pentatonic and blues
scales are even common

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.270
throughout heavy metal music.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.550
("Highway to Hell" by AC/DC)

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:40.960
Country music, too.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.270
In fact, country is
thought to be the result

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.240
of blues music mixing with folk music

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.470
brought by European immigrants to the U.S.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.920
Because of this, many
country songs are written

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.140
with a blues scale

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.586
or following the 12-bar blues progression.

00:09:53.586 --> 00:09:58.586
("Folsom Prison Blues" by Johnny Cash)

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.750
Blues, here we go.
- Blues.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:04.400
- I was thinking, we don't
have to do classic blues.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.710
- We'll just have our own twist to it.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:07.543
- That's what it is.

00:10:07.543 --> 00:10:08.960
We just gotta let creation happen.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.200
- I mean, we always do.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.790
So our song contains the
12-bar blues chord progression

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.570
as well as the blues scale,

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:17.470
and some other elements
of the blues were included

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:19.270
but with our own twist.

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.170
For example, the wailing type of sound

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.700
of the bottleneck slide
guitar got translated

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.200
into this type of synth
sound (plays synth sound),

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:32.940
which I think has a squiggle
and has that wailing quality.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.710
And obviously, the other
parts (adds more sounds)

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.820
are quite not traditional,

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.310
but we tried to go with sounds that have

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:45.164
this laid back, yet
yearning, type of quality.

00:10:45.164 --> 00:10:49.664
(blues music with synth incorporated)

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.000
- Let us know what you think
of our blues-inspired song,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.502
and make sure you don't
forget to subscribe.

00:11:37.502 --> 00:11:39.418
- Three is the one.
- Three is the one, yeah.

00:11:39.418 --> 00:11:42.001
(bright chord)

